// Two enums that share a tag width share a C spelling, and until 0.9.102 that made them interchangeable
// at every hand-off. The kind rule reads both sides as `Num` and passes; every numeric rule reads the
// typedef name as "not a number I know" and takes its documented silent path. Neither is wrong on its
// own terms — nothing owned type IDENTITY.
//
// `W` is deliberately a DIFFERENT width from `A`: the crossing is not a widening that happens to be
// safe, it is a value of one type arriving where a value of another was declared.
type enum A : uint8  { A0, A1, A2 }
type enum B : uint8  { B0, B1, B2 }
type enum W : uint32 { W0, W1, W2 }

fn int32 main()
{
    A a = A::A2;
    B b = a;
    W w = a;
    return 0;
}
